WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.919
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.219 --> 00:00:09.884
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.184 --> 00:00:17.016
Unit seventeen: Human Trafficking. Today you will focus on victims with passive force, make and allow.

4
00:00:17.316 --> 00:00:19.899
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:23.799
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:25.732
Forced labor.

7
00:00:26.717 --> 00:00:31.143
Forced labor means work that a person is made to do under threat and without fair pay.

8
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.260
Forced labor was found on the fishing boats.

9
00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.735
Your turn. Say: Forced labor.

10
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:41.706
Forced labor.

11
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:43.707
Trafficking victim.

12
00:00:44.717 --> 00:00:49.328
Trafficking victim means a person who is recruited or moved in order to be exploited.

13
00:00:49.917 --> 00:00:52.323
The trafficking victim received medical care.

14
00:00:52.917 --> 00:00:56.115
Your turn. Say: Trafficking victim.

15
00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:00.386
Trafficking victim.

16
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:02.256
Exploitation.

17
00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:07.421
Exploitation means treating people unfairly to gain profit from their work.

18
00:01:08.017 --> 00:01:11.119
Exploitation often begins with false promises.

19
00:01:11.717 --> 00:01:14.497
Your turn. Say: Exploitation.

20
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:18.497
Exploitation.

21
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.006
Anti-trafficking law.

22
00:01:22.017 --> 00:01:26.710
Anti-trafficking law means a law that punishes traffickers and protects victims.

23
00:01:27.317 --> 00:01:30.814
The anti-trafficking law raised prison sentences.

24
00:01:31.417 --> 00:01:34.870
Your turn. Say: Anti-trafficking law.

25
00:01:37.733 --> 00:01:39.327
Anti-trafficking law.

26
00:01:40.083 --> 00:01:41.456
Modern slavery.

27
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:46.911
Modern slavery means hidden forms of forced work and control that exist today.

28
00:01:47.517 --> 00:01:50.143
Modern slavery affects millions worldwide.

29
00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:54.227
Your turn. Say: Modern slavery.

30
00:01:57.067 --> 00:01:58.590
Modern slavery.

31
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:00.757
Trafficking network.

32
00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:06.164
Trafficking network means an organised group that moves people for exploitation.

33
00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.935
Police broke up a trafficking network.

34
00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.783
Your turn. Say: Trafficking network.

35
00:02:15.633 --> 00:02:17.784
Trafficking network.

36
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:19.748
Victim support.

37
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:26.314
Victim support means help such as shelter, counselling and legal advice for survivors.

38
00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:29.323
Victim support helped her rebuild her life.

39
00:02:29.917 --> 00:02:32.999
Your turn. Say: Victim support.

40
00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:37.264
Victim support.

41
00:02:38.017 --> 00:02:39.935
International cooperation.

42
00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:45.295
International cooperation means countries working together to reach a shared goal.

43
00:02:45.883 --> 00:02:48.916
International cooperation led to the arrests.

44
00:02:49.517 --> 00:02:52.993
Your turn. Say: International cooperation.

45
00:02:55.833 --> 00:02:58.158
International cooperation.

46
00:02:58.967 --> 00:03:02.884
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:08.945
Visal, a report says 'Victims are forced to work without pay.' What structure is this?

48
00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:23.943
It is the passive of force + object + to-infinitive. Active: 'Traffickers force victims to work.' The object becomes the subject, and the agent can be omitted: Victims are forced to work.

49
00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.061
Does 'make' follow the same pattern?

50
00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:39.840
Almost. Active 'make' has no 'to': 'They made her work.' But the passive needs 'to': 'She was made to work.' 'Let' has no normal passive, so we say 'was allowed to'.

51
00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:42.950
Why is this structure useful?

52
00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:56.527
It focuses on the victim and the harm, not on the trafficker. It is common in legal and news reports: 'Many are forced, deceived or pressured into leaving home.'

53
00:03:57.667 --> 00:03:59.376
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:00.217 --> 00:04:04.736
Grammar focus: Passive with force and make and allow plus to-infinitive.

55
00:04:05.036 --> 00:04:06.257
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:09.263
Victims are forced to work without pay.

57
00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.463
She was made to hand over her passport.

58
00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.302
Survivors should be allowed to stay and receive support.

59
00:04:17.267 --> 00:04:26.992
Active: make + object + base verb (They made her work). Passive: be made + to-infinitive (She was made to work). Use be allowed to, not be let to.

60
00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:31.192
Be careful! Don't say: She was made work all night.

61
00:04:33.133 --> 00:04:35.690
Say: She was made to work all night.

62
00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:38.326
The passive of make needs to + verb.

63
00:04:39.133 --> 00:04:41.728
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:42.533 --> 00:04:43.918
Behind the Hidden Crime.

65
00:04:44.218 --> 00:04:48.179
Modern slavery hides in farms, factories and homes in every region.

66
00:04:49.167 --> 00:04:55.659
A trafficking victim is often forced to work long hours for no pay, and exploitation is kept secret through threats.

67
00:04:56.667 --> 00:05:01.743
Trafficking networks cross borders, so police in different countries must share information.

68
00:05:02.733 --> 00:05:07.868
Anti-trafficking law should ensure that survivors are allowed to stay and receive victim support.

69
00:05:08.867 --> 00:05:12.608
Forced labor ends only when governments and communities work together.

70
00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:17.818
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:18.683 --> 00:05:20.950
The workers blank to sign the contract.

72
00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.729
A, were made. B, were made sign. C, made.

73
00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:30.104
The answer is A: were made.

74
00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:32.924
Which sentence is correct?

75
00:05:33.224 --> 00:05:38.434
A, She was let to leave.. B, She was allowed to leave.. C, She was allowed leave..

76
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.255
The answer is B: She was allowed to leave..

77
00:05:45.417 --> 00:05:48.972
Work done under threat and without fair pay is blank labor.

78
00:05:49.272 --> 00:05:52.531
A, forced. B, skilled. C, paid.

79
00:05:56.067 --> 00:05:57.753
The answer is A: forced.

80
00:05:58.917 --> 00:06:02.960
An organised group that moves victims across borders is a trafficking blank.

81
00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:06.855
A, network. B, reserve. C, bloc.

82
00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.318
The answer is A: network.

83
00:06:13.483 --> 00:06:17.050
According to the reading, how is exploitation kept secret?

84
00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:21.840
A, through threats. B, through advertising. C, through tariffs.

85
00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.446
The answer is A: through threats.

86
00:06:28.633 --> 00:06:31.785
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:37.366
Why does fighting human trafficking require cooperation across many different countries?

88
00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:47.604
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:47.904 --> 00:07:14.966
Human trafficking requires cooperation because victims are often recruited in one country, moved through another and exploited in a third. Traffickers use these borders to avoid arrest. Countries must share intelligence, harmonise anti-trafficking laws and protect victims wherever they are found. Without international cooperation, criminals simply move to the weakest place.

90
00:07:15.266 --> 00:07:16.894
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:17.783 --> 00:07:23.547
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive with force and make and allow plus to-infinitive.

92
00:07:23.847 --> 00:07:29.147
Today's value is human dignity. Every person deserves respect wherever they are.

93
00:07:29.447 --> 00:07:32.285
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:32.585 --> 00:07:35.585
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

